0, "poll_margin": -0.85, "poll_sd": 6.01, "poll_weight": 0.41, "fundamentals": {"margin": -2.81, "lean": -8.0, "environment": 7.38, "incumbency": -3.0, "money": 0.81}, "rating_margin": -8.1, "sigma_race": 5.5, "sigma_total": 6.23, "baseline_margin": -3.88, "analyst_adjustment": -0.62, "key_factors": ["South Carolina's Republican lean (PVI R+8) anchors the race despite tight recent polls", "Prediction markets price Andrews at only 14-17%, far below the polling average", "Andrews has a large fundraising edge ($10.8M vs. $347K), but outside groups have spent $2.6M backing Graham", "Ratings split between Lean R and Likely R, with no major rater calling it a Tossup"], "rationale": "Recent polls show a surprisingly tight race, with Graham leading by only one to three points in September surveys, and Andrews has dramatically out-raised the incumbent. But the state's strong Republican baseline, heavy outside spending for Graham, and markets pricing Andrews around 15% all point to a modest but real Republican edge, landing near a four-to-five-point Graham margin.", "watch": "A late debate moment or further Democratic spending could tighten the race, but Andrews would need to convert polling parity into actual votes in a state that has not elected a Democrat to the Senate in decades.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Incumbent Darline Graham (R) is running."]}, "overview": "Appointed incumbent Darline Graham defends the seat against Democrat Annie Andrews in a state that leans Republican by about eight points. Despite that baseline, the race has drawn unexpected attention: September polls show Graham ahead by only one to three points, and Andrews has raised over $10 million to Graham's roughly $350,000. Outside groups have spent heavily to back Graham, offsetting his weak fundraising.\n\nExpert raters mostly call the race Lean or Likely Republican, and prediction markets give Andrews only about a 15% chance of winning. The polling may overstate Andrews's support given several surveys come from partisan sponsors, but the race is closer than South Carolina Senate contests typically are. Graham remains favored by roughly four to five points.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.1565, "margin": -5.15}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.2122, "margin": -5.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.2115, "margin": -5.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.2143, "margin": -5.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.1474, "margin": -5.5}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.2135, "margin": -4.0}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.2708, "margin": -4.0}, {"date": "2026-09-29", "p_dem": 0.2623, "margin": -4.0}, {"date": "2026-09-30", "p_dem": 0.2878, "margin": -3.5}, {"date": "2026-10-01", "p_dem": 0.2627, "margin": -4.0}, {"date": "2026-10-02", "p_dem": 0.2896, "margin": -3.5}, {"date": "2026-10-03", "p_dem": 0.2599, "margin": -4.0}, {"date": "2026-10-04", "p_dem": 0.2566, "margin": -4.0}, {"date": "2026-10-05", "p_dem": 0.194, "margin": -4.5}, {"date": "2026-10-06", "p_dem": 0.1953, "margin": -4.5}]}